  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">A key objective of the UNFPA Supplies Partnership (Phase 3) is to advance a transition to domestic financing for essential sexual and reproductive health commodities. This helps to ensure national governments prioritise the realisation of universal sexual and reproductive health. and that financing is more sustainable.  In 2022, UNFPA Supplies&apos; work resulted in the Government of Papua New Guinea making its first domestic financial contribution to family planning commodities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA) Supplies Partnership is committed to ensuring that women and girls have access to a variety of high-quality reproductive health commodities, including family planning options, whenever they desire or require them. To achieve this, the Partnership provides a range of contraception options and ensures adequate training and support for providers across the Pacific. In 2024, this included the introduction of a new three- monthly contraceptive method with a potential for self- injection to the Essential Medicines List in three countries, Solomon Islands, Marshall Islands and Kiribati, which marks a significant expansion in the availability and choice of contraceptive methods in the Pacific. The introduction was supported by training for nurses, midwives and clinical officers (27 female and 3 male) in Solomon Islands and Fiji to enhance provider capacity and empower women and girls to make informed decisions, and strengthened logistics for reliable supply, monitoring and zero stock outs at service delivery points</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA) Supplies Partnership supports the development of a sustainable country-defined, country-driven and country-funded process to improve the availability of key reproductive health commodities to the last mile, ensuring people have access to a range of quality assured products, including family planning options. This includes support to Ministries of Health across the Pacific to strengthen capacity in logistics, quantification and supply chain management to ensure that countries don&#x2019;t experience stockouts of supplies and that supplies are available when and where they are needed. To enhance the capacity of service delivery personnel in data collection and reporting, UNFPA Supplies is rolling out the Contraceptive Logistics Management System (CLMS) across the Pacific. Implementing CLMS standard operating procedures across 7 countries in 2024, UNFPA Supplies delivered Training of Trainers and training for service delivery points in Fiji, Solomon Islands, Kiribati, and Tonga. Additionally, tools have been supplied to facilitate implementation in Fiji, Kiribati and Tonga. In total, 30 Master Trainers were trained (17 female and 13 male), 3 from Fiji, 13 from the Solomon Islands, 9 from Kiribati, and 5 from Tonga, along with 64 personnel from health facilities</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
